INT. DRAINED SWIMMING POOL - TABLE TENNIS HALL - DAY

A municipal pool, long since emptied, its blue tiles chipped to the colour of old teeth. Two green tables stand in the deep end where the water once was. Dust turns in a shaft of light from a skylight.

DEZ OKAFOR (late 20s, loose-limbed, a towel around his neck) bounces the little white ball on his paddle, humming. Across the table, LIN HUA (teens, tight ponytail, fierce eyes) waits, perfectly still. In the shallow end bleachers, GRANDPA TSUBASA (80s, folded in a cardigan) eats tangerine segments. At the net post, REFEREE BARNABY (60s, clipboard, whistle) checks his watch with a priest's solemnity.

BARNABY
Match point, Lin. Take your time.

DEZ
Lin, nobody's rushing you. We could be at a picnic.

LIN
Don't talk to me like I'm eight, Dez.

DEZ
Wouldn't dream of it.

He serves, soft and lazy. Lin returns it just as soft. The rally floats back and forth, almost tender, ball ticking in the quiet like a slow clock.

TSUBASA
Easy, Lin-chan. Easy.

DEZ
(over the ball)
Your grandfather's a good coach.

LIN
(at the same time)
Why are you letting me win?

DEZ
(at the same time, softer)
I'm not letting you.

^LIN
You are. You've dropped four easy balls in a row.

Dez mishits on purpose. The ball skitters into the deep corner. The hall holds its breath.

Barnaby lowers his clipboard and looks, very slowly, at Tsubasa. The old man stops chewing.

TSUBASA
Barnaby. Tell her about the envelope.
